    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 141749\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n949 AM PST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Lingering showers and breezes exit into the afternoon, leading\n  to calmer weather for the rest of the weekend.\n\n* Additional precipitation and cooler weather arrives Monday and \n  Tuesday and again in the second half of next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through Friday\n\nWinds are easing across much of the region with a few favored\npeaks and passes still seeing hazardously strong winds. These \nwinds look to reduce in the mid to late morning while the last few\nshowers exit to the east. Snow chances across the Sierra reduce \nthrough the morning as well. Mostly calm conditions are expected \nfor the rest of Saturday and through Sunday as a ridge builds over\nthe region.\n\nConfidence is strong for a return to unsettled weather for Monday\nas a low pressure builds into the area early that morning and \nspreads through the day. The trough associated with this low \npressure will deepen over our area, pulling a better feed of \nmoisture after it crosses the Sierra, limiting Sierra snow \npotential, but leading to better rainfall chances for the rest of \nthe region. Rain looks to become widespread into Monday afternoon \nfor the areas east of the Sierra Nevada, but rain rates look to \nstay light. Rainfall totals have reduced over recent model \nupdates, with showers being a bit more scattered: Expect around \n0.02 to 0.20\" for all but Northeast Clark Co, Mohave Co, and \nLincoln Co which will range from 0.30 to 0.60\" after showers exit \nto the east. Wednesday will offer another pause in rain but \nanother batch of showers arrives early Thursday and could linger \ninto next Friday but these showers look to be very scattered and \nshort lived.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast \nPackage...Northerly winds of around 10 knots last into the mid \nafternoon, then weaken and shift to the northeast. Light \nnorthwesterly winds develop this evening. FEW to SCT mid and high \nclouds last through most of the forecast period with bases at or \nabove 15kft. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Breezy northerly to \nnorthwesterly winds continue in the Las Vegas Valley into the \nevening with speeds around 10 knots. Meanwhile, gusty westerly winds \nat KDAG will shift to the north and weaken, but sustained speeds \nremain around 10 knots. Lighter winds under 10 knots can be expected \nin the Colorado River Valley and Owens Valley through the rest of \nthe day. Lower cloud cover in southern San Bernardino County and \nMohave County will gradually clear out this morning and afternoon. \nOtherwise, FEW to SCT mid and high clouds last through most of the \nforecast period with bases at or above 15kft. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Murdock\nAVIATION...Meltzer\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
